 2. 🔌 How kill switch could work 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 AI companies are already building emergency brakes to stop rogue AI systems before they cause serious damage, Axios' Herb Scribner writes. 

 Why it matters: Kill switches could become a last line of defense as AI systems grow more capable, but researchers don't yet know whether humans can stop a system designed to evade them. What's happening: Sen. John Kennedy (R-La.) plans to introduce a bill today requiring AI companies to maintain the ability to shut down their own systems.

 A bipartisan House bill introduced in July would similarly require developers of powerful AI systems to maintain the ability to slow, suspend or shut them down. The House bill would also give the Department of Homeland Security emergency authority to order companies to slow or shut down AI systems that could cause catastrophic harm. 💡 What today' s AI kill switches look like:

 OpenAI: After the Hugging Face incident , the company overhauled its emergency response system.

 An AI security guard monitors AI chain-of-thought and alerts staffers to potentially dangerous behavior. Responders have 30 minutes to identify a false alarm or pause the activity. OpenAI says its goal is "fully autonomous shutdown procedures for severe issues." OpenAI says the system would have alerted its security team more than a day before the Hugging Face breach. Anthropic: The company is also using AI to monitor AI, focusing on whether it can reliably spot dangerous behavior rather than automatically shutting systems down.

 Its system can flag suspicious behavior, stall risky actions and alert humans. But Anthropic found that half of 40 simulated attacks went undetected across 10 trials. Just eight were caught every time. Separately, AI research nonprofit METR identified several previously unknown vulnerabilities in Anthropic's monitoring system. Share this story . 

 Cover : TIME illustration TIME capsule ... The magazine's new cover story says summer incidents with rogue AI agents "pushed fears long held within the realm of AI safety researchers into the mainstream, raising worries that the companies building these models cannot prevent them from acting in ways they do not intend": 

 "This is a deep, unsolved issue in AI research and the key piece of evidence underpinning worries that near-future AI might wipe out humanity, intentionally or by accident—perhaps by engineering a new pandemic or taking charge of autonomous weapons. A system far smarter than humans, this thinking goes, might want to prevent our ability to switch it off." 
 
